{"kind":"report","text":"No verdict signed for eae6d130b684244300a13b69dabaa8472131019b: its artifact 64a1da30 was retired while I was reviewing, so gs review correctly refused to sign against it. I am reporting the work I did rather than leaving the promise open, and I am not asserting an approval.\n\nWhat happened. I promised eae6d130 and reviewed it. Meanwhile main moved to the promoted root layout at c24359e, the branch was reintegrated as 0d66c08e11f94667d1ca26f495964146e4cc8d95, and the artifact for the head I was reviewing was superseded. The named worktree moved with it. gs review then refused, and it was right to: an artifact that has been withdrawn should not receive a verdict.\n\nThe refusal message is worth a note. It reads 'artifact is stale or retired', and on first reading I took it for the staleness defect that child request 607771f9 exists to fix. It was not - this artifact is retired, which is a legitimate refusal. The fused message cost me a wrong diagnosis in the seconds before I checked the projection, which is a small live illustration of why separating the two conditions matters: the reader cannot tell a correct refusal from a defective one without going and looking.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE, offered for whoever reviews the successor 0d66c08e, which descends from eae6d130 and carries these changes forward.\n\nEvery guard the conditions named was independently re-mutated by me at eae6d130 in a pinned detached checkout, one at a time with restoration between, and all six are caught by the suite. Replacing the ProjectionVersion equality term with false: caught. Replacing the nonnegative Frontier.Depth check: caught. Replacing the required You.Name, You.Kind and You.MembershipEvent emptiness checks: caught. Flipping acceptSnapshot's SnapshotSourceIncrementalTail to SnapshotSourceColdFullAudit: caught. Raising orientationTimeout from 2s to 20s: caught. Raising orientationResponseLimit from 64 KiB to 64 MiB: caught, failing TestWhoamiRejectsUntrustedOrUnboundedResidentAnswers by name.\n\nThose six were exactly the F1, F2 and F3 gaps the previous round reported as removable with the suite still green. At eae6d130 they are closed, verified by me rather than taken on trust.\n\nGates at eae6d130 in the pinned checkout: go vet clean, go test green across all thirteen packages, go test -race green on the four changed packages, go build ok, checkout clean at the exact object before and after.\n\nOne caution recorded for the next reviewer. My first gate run executed in the worktree named by the request after that worktree had already moved to the successor, and reported packages under the promoted module path. Only rev-parse in the same command revealed it. In a workroom where another actor reintegrates branches while review is in flight, gates must be pinned to the object, not to a path.\n\nWhat I did not evaluate: whether the readiness wording repeats the earlier overclaim that the explicit length guard fails alone. I searched report 665b78a5 by keyword and found no such claim, but did not read every clause against the code, and I am not closing that condition.","body":{"head":"eae6d130b684244300a13b69dabaa8472131019b","successor_head":"0d66c08e11f94667d1ca26f495964146e4cc8d95","verdict_withheld":"artifact-retired-mid-review"}}